{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"PMCID:PMC3105993","internalQuery":"PMCID:PMC3105993","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":1,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"21673804","source":"MED","pmid":"21673804","pmcid":"PMC3105993","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3105993"]},"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0020459","title":"The effectiveness of RNAi in Caenorhabditis elegans is maintained during spaceflight.","authorString":"Etheridge T, Nemoto K, Hashizume T, Mori C, Sugimoto T, Suzuki H, Fukui K, Yamazaki T, Higashibata A, Szewczyk NJ, Higashitani A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Etheridge T","lastName":"Etheridge","firstName":"Timothy","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3588-8711"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Clinical Physiology, Royal Derby Hospital, University of Nottingham, Derby, England."}]}},{"fullName":"Nemoto K","lastName":"Nemoto","firstName":"Kanako","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Hashizume T","lastName":"Hashizume","firstName":"Toko","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Mori C","lastName":"Mori","firstName":"Chihiro","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Sugimoto T","lastName":"Sugimoto","firstName":"Tomoko","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Suzuki H","lastName":"Suzuki","firstName":"Hiromi","initials":"H"},{"fullName":"Fukui K","lastName":"Fukui","firstName":"Keiji","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Yamazaki T","lastName":"Yamazaki","firstName":"Takashi","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Higashibata A","lastName":"Higashibata","firstName":"Akira","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Szewczyk NJ","lastName":"Szewczyk","firstName":"Nathaniel J","initials":"NJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4425-9746"}},{"fullName":"Higashitani A","lastName":"Higashitani","firstName":"Atsushi","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6920-0594"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6920-0594"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3588-8711"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4425-9746"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"6","journalIssueId":1854259,"dateOfPublication":"2011 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2011,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"PLoS One","title":"PloS one","essn":"1932-6203","issn":"1932-6203","isoabbreviation":"PLoS One","nlmid":"101285081"},"printPublicationDate":"2011-01-01"},"pubYear":"2011","pageInfo":"e20459","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Overcoming spaceflight-induced (patho)physiologic adaptations is a major challenge preventing long-term deep space exploration. RNA interference (RNAi) has emerged as a promising therapeutic for combating diseases on Earth; however the efficacy of RNAi in space is currently unknown.<h4>Methods</h4>Caenorhabditis elegans were prepared in liquid media on Earth using standard techniques and treated acutely with RNAi or a vector control upon arrival in Low Earth Orbit. After culturing during 4 and 8 d spaceflight, experiments were stopped by freezing at -80°C until analysis by mRNA and microRNA array chips, microscopy and Western blot on return to Earth. Ground controls (GC) on Earth were simultaneously grown under identical conditions.<h4>Results</h4>After 8 d spaceflight, mRNA expression levels of components of the RNAi machinery were not different from that in GC (e.g., Dicer, Argonaute, Piwi; P>0.05). The expression of 228 microRNAs, of the 232 analysed, were also unaffected during 4 and 8 d spaceflight (P>0.05). In spaceflight, RNAi against green fluorescent protein (gfp) reduced chromosomal gfp expression in gonad tissue, which was not different from GC. RNAi against rbx-1 also induced abnormal chromosome segregation in the gonad during spaceflight as on Earth. Finally, culture in RNAi against lysosomal cathepsins prevented degradation of the muscle-specific α-actin protein in both spaceflight and GC conditions.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Treatment with RNAi works as effectively in the space environment as on Earth within multiple tissues, suggesting RNAi may provide an effective tool for combating spaceflight-induced pathologies aboard future long-duration space missions. Furthermore, this is the first demonstration that RNAi can be utilised to block muscle protein degradation, both on Earth and in space.","affiliation":"Division of Clinical Physiology, Royal Derby Hospital, University of Nottingham, Derby, England.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Medical Research Council","acronym":"MRC_","grantId":"G0801271","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAMS NIH HHS","acronym":"AR","grantId":"R01 AR054342","orderIn":0},{"agency":"PHS HHS","grantId":"ARO54342","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Lysosomes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"RE","qualifierName":"radiation effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Caenorhabditis elegans","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"RE","qualifierName":"radiation effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Peptide Hydrolases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DF","qualifierName":"deficiency"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majo